# Unit Testing

Tests cover **non-configuration logic in every backend tier**. Configuration and wiring stay out of scope.

## Test Project

- `NTierTemplate.Test/` references **Domain**, **Application**, **Data**, **Api**, **Cli**, and **Queue**
- Do not reference `ntier-template-web/`
- Organize tests by **layer folder** (matching project name), then **domain folder** — e.g. `NTierTemplate.Test/NTierTemplate.Application/Users/UserApplicationServiceTests.cs`
- Shared fixtures live in `NTierTemplate.Test/Support/`

## What to Test

- Domain entity behavior, message mapping, and principal helpers
- Application service orchestration and queue command processing
- Data DAO algorithms and `UnitOfWork` transaction boundaries (in-memory EF + Identity)
- Api JWT/token and HTTP adapter branching (mock application services)
- Cli and Queue domain handlers and scoped host adapters where they contain logic

## What Not to Test

- Configuration and wiring: IoC registration, `Program.cs`, host bootstrap, `appsettings`, EF entity `Configure()` only, migrations
- Pure DTOs/property bags with no branching
- Third-party infrastructure adapters without a seam (for example raw SMTP send) unless an integration test is explicitly added

## Conventions

- Mock application and DAO **interfaces** in Application and Api tests — use **Moq**
- Use **in-memory EF** for Data-layer DAO and `UnitOfWork` tests
- Use **FluentAssertions** for expectations
- When a test namespace nests under `NTierTemplate.Test.NTierTemplate.*`, qualify domain types with `global::NTierTemplate.Users.User` (and similar) to avoid namespace shadowing
- Keep test types and methods domain-named, matching the code under test

